@hamza-m-masood @hasanfaesal Thanks for sharing!  I'm curious if there were some other steps left out or something... how did either of you get this working as-is?

> ```
> {
>   keybinds: {
>     input_submit: return,
>     input_newline: shift+return
>   }
> }
> ```

Aside from the missing quote marks, in `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` it's against the schema and I get an error `Property keybinds is not allowed.`

According to https://opencode.ai/docs/keybinds/ it belongs in `tui.json`, but even when I put this:

```json
{
    "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/tui.json",
    "keybinds": {
        "input_newline": "shift+return",
        "input_submit": "return"
    }
}
```

...in `~/.config/opencode/tui.json` (which didn't exist beforehand), and add the other two lines to `tmux.conf` (which came with one other line already, `set -ga terminal-overrides ',xterm*:smcup@:rmcup@'`) and source the update as stated, it unfortunately doesn't work when I reattach, even if I create a new tmux window and open a fresh opencode.

Came across this issue as well. It happened when I switched terminal emulators on my Mac from iterm2 to Ghostty. The thing that I noticed was that when I examined ~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json I had 2 tokens there a "github-copilot-enterprise" one and a separate "github-copilot" one.

```
{
  "github-copilot-enterprise": {
    "type": "oauth",
    "refresh": "blahblahredacted",
    "access": "blahblahredacted",
    "expires": 0,
    "enterpriseUrl": "blahblahredacted.ghe.com"
  },
  "github-copilot": {
    "type": "oauth",
    "access": "blahblahredacted",
    "refresh": "blahblahredacted",
    "expires": 0
  }
}
``` 
I think when connecting the second terminal instance it created the "github-copilot" auth block which is taking precedence. I removed the file and reran /connect and got an auth.json like this.
```
{
  "github-copilot": {
    "type": "oauth",
    "access": "blahblahredacted",
    "refresh": "blahblahredacted",
    "expires": 0
  }
}%
```
I manually added the "enterpriseUrl": "blahblahredacted.ghe.com" field into that json and after restarting copilot the connection error went away

@Fuzu This all sounds like awesome progress, but to be honest, I don't trust closed-source projects as there is no ability for code-audit by the community.

And it costing money also rubs me the wrong way because this is essentially a fork with extra improvements of an open-source project that is free, without any back-contribution, benefiting off the foundation that OpenCode built.

That said, you're fully within your rights to do this according to OpenCode's license, but that doesn't mean it's gonna vibe with the open source community. Keep up the good work though, and I hope some of your improvements are ported back to OpenCode in the future.
